  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWarm air advection continues tonight, helping to keep overnight\nlows some 10 to 15 degrees above normal. Enough of a puff in \nthe boundary layer should mitigate river valley fog, save for \nperhaps the Tygart Valley. Attention then turns to a cold front \nsagging into the middle and upper Ohio Valley during the day \ntomorrow. Several of the CAMS are showing some robust \ndevelopment along and ahead of the front across our area \ncoinciding with max heating and enhanced by anomously high \ndewpoints for this time of year. SPC maintains a marginal risk \nfor severe weather (level 1 ) for most with damaging winds the \nprimary threat given the increase in deep layer sheer and \nsteepening lapse rates. \n\nWhile the storm potential will fade tomorrow night, the front\nwill linger with the potential for rounds of showers traversing\nthe same areas. This could lead to isolated flooding concerns\nconsidering the anomalous PWATS in place. Confidence on where \nthis occurs is not particularly high, though southeast OH, \nnortheast KY and central/northern WV are the areas where there \nare some model convergence. WPC continues the marginal risk \n(level 1) for excessive rainfall across this corridor. \n\nThe front will gradually return north as warm front during the\nday Thursday with lingering clouds and few showers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nSoutherly flow strengthens Thursday night ahead of a strong cold\nfront. This will likely keep temperatures overnight resembling\naverage daytime highs for this time of year. Gusty winds will\ndevelop particularly across the hilltops and ridgetops with this\nspreading into the lower elevations by Friday morning. Gusts to\n35 kts are possible on the highest ridges in the northern\nmountains with 20 to 25 kts for ths hilltops and ridges\nelsewhere. \n\nThe front will race across the area as the day progresses Friday\nwith gusty showers followed by much colder air. These frontal winds\nmay be strong enough to cause some downed limbs and power hits.\nGusty winds will continue behind the front as well. A wind \nadvisory may eventually be needed in the mountains given the \nstrength of cold air advection. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nShowery upslope enhanced light precipitation may linger Friday \nnight into early Saturday across the northern lowlands and \nespecially the northern mountains. Across the higher elevations\nthis may be realized as snow showers, though no accumulation is\nexpected. The bigger story will be the cold with daytime highs\nexpected to be roughly 15 degrees below normal. The threat for\nfrost across the lowlands and a freeze in the mountains\ncontinues to be advertised for Sunday morning as high pressure\nsettles overhead.\n\nA slow moderating trend will ensue Sunday and early next week\nthough high pressure will remain in control.",
